New order method breaks external CryptoProvider implementations

Adding an abstract method to this public SPI means any existing third-party `CryptoProvider` on the classpath that was compiled against the previous interface will fail when `CryptoIntegration.detectProvider()` sorts providers and invokes `CryptoProvider::order`. Providing a default keeps binary/source compatibility while still allowing built-in providers to override their priority.

Proposed patch:
```diff
--- a/common/src/main/java/org/keycloak/common/crypto/CryptoProvider.java
+++ b/common/src/main/java/org/keycloak/common/crypto/CryptoProvider.java
@@ -36,13 +36,13 @@ public interface CryptoProvider {
      */
     Provider getBouncyCastleProvider();

     /**
      * Order of this provider. This allows to specify which CryptoProvider will have preference in case that more of them are on the classpath.
      *
      * The higher number has preference over the lower number
      */
-    int order();
+    default int order() { return 0; }

     /**
      * Get some algorithm provider implementation. Returned implementation can be dependent according to if we have
      * non-fips bouncycastle or fips bouncycastle on the classpath.

```

Default-strategy upgrade tests still omit rolling-updates

`createInitialDeployment` returns immediately when `updateStrategy` is null, before the newly added feature flag is set. The parameterized default-strategy cases therefore still deploy a Keycloak CR without `rolling-updates` enabled, which defeats the new feature gate and can make the null-strategy rolling-upgrade test fail for the wrong reason. Enable the feature before the early return so both strategy variants exercise the gated code path.

Proposed patch:
```diff
